{"title":"2025 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 17","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(substitution)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (substitution)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.1_(alternative_using_shoelace_formula_for_last_step)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.1 (alternative using shoelace formula for last step)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.2_(alternative_using_triangle_in_-plane_for_last_two_steps)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.2 (alternative using triangle in -plane for last two steps)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(directly_finding_the_roots)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (directly finding the roots)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_Continuum_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by Continuum Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_4_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 4 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Fast_Video_Solution_by_Veer_Mahajan\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fast Video Solution by Veer Mahajan</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c9e671305afcec8d221594f6d2b8965c69d8fbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z + i)(z + 2i)(z + 3i) + 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"216\" height=\"20\" /> has three roots in the complex plane, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/c/dac5dec897250384833e3c1b44639bf91cca823a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = \\sqrt{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"21\" />. What is the area of the triangle formed by these three roots?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/b/a2b393f523575e10899e4cb084e8c20e8a1ffbe1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}~6 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}~8 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}~10 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}~12 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}~14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"378\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28substitution.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(substitution)\">Solution 1 (substitution)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (substitution)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We initially note the symmetry of the product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/7/3476b647f6eeef47a0c0ea1b26d4cd248dab53b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+i)(z+2i)(z+3i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"176\" height=\"20\" />, in that the factors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/c/51c60511b4af0aa934acc01f0559a135264c4e06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/2/8521f69fbca190697ef10117f0047e325d9aaaf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+3i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad290b5c5196970e516d54c6df83441d883ad64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+2i) \\mp i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"91\" height=\"20\" /> respectively. Accordingly, making the substitution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/0/d10604df05dfe856e23607900d4acff326630d10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w = z+2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" /> enables us to apply the 'difference of two squares' identity, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/e/19eb9bf4b8786b7859fe47d5828a644074cb318e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}(z+i)(z+2i)(z+3i)+10 &amp;= (w-i)(w)(w+i)+10 \\\\ &amp;= w\\left(w^2-i^2\\right)+10 \\\\ &amp;= w\\left(w^2+1\\right)+10 = w^3+w+10.\\end{align*}\" width=\"487\" height=\"75\" />\nHaving thus eliminated the quadratic term of the given cubic polynomial, we easily observe that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> is a root of this new cubic (in particular, by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_root_theorem\" title=\"Rational root theorem\">rational root theorem</a>, the only possible rational roots are integers that are factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />).\n</p><p>It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/4/b749d981f307ad21306b5868206a0eff3bc1b350.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^3+w+10 = \\left(w+2\\right)\\left(w^2-2w+5\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"299\" height=\"23\" />, and hence, using either the quadratic formula or completing the square, the other two roots are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0b8009df368d0bdeb0e40a697c49babde402094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf1294c23fe3c251dfda2bdcf0240c9822fac7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />. Moreover, since our initial substitution simply shifts all points in the complex plane by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/a/9cacfe19783fb9a654bbf3e2d4c55db87fae930b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> (i.e. by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> units vertically upward) and area is preserved under translation, the required area of the triangle in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-plane is the same as that of the triangle formed by these three roots in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />-plane.\n</p><p>Now, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0b8009df368d0bdeb0e40a697c49babde402094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf1294c23fe3c251dfda2bdcf0240c9822fac7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> of course have the same real part, the side of the triangle between these two vertices is simply vertical, and has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/5/9c5373f50ef131fe298a1ceb85ef69d75f6d3deb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2-(-2) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />. The length of the altitude to that side is the perpendicular distance from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> to the vertical line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/7/1b78cb2f1d8a308eeb34e14c27b7fa436e1e450a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\usepackage{physics}\\Re(z) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" />, i.e. the horizontal distance from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/b/77b2468395eef65bdd7ab1f4624bb21423dad508.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(-2) = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />. Thus the area of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfd7edb7a737843d03df1c08a28866c1a1d1aad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"139\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~lprado\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_1.1_.28alternative_using_shoelace_formula_for_last_step.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.1_(alternative_using_shoelace_formula_for_last_step)\">Solution 1.1 (alternative using shoelace formula for last step)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.1 (alternative using shoelace formula for last step)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>While the area of the triangle (once we have determined its vertices) can be straightforwardly computed using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/1/86132adb4e8897be3465ac3fa023bb7ccf82be5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{area } = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{base} \\cdot \\text{perpendicular height}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"283\" height=\"38\" /> as explained above, it is also possible to use the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Shoelace_Theorem\" title=\"Shoelace Theorem\">shoelace formula</a>, a general result giving the area of any polygon in the plane in terms of the coordinates of its vertices.\n</p><p>Specifically, the vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0b8009df368d0bdeb0e40a697c49babde402094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf1294c23fe3c251dfda2bdcf0240c9822fac7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> in the complex plane of course become the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6525419c2f6d4228feb404b8627fd6b55ca142d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-2,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/b/1bb913ad463dcc2916d4135977f0d06f20ad93cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/3/6b3d5bc190023f3a0f2dfec84450d74df080d8ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,-2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" /> in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> plane, so the shoelace formula gives the area of the triangle as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/1/0c1daa28db9635e9b13612c3557779a51d99fc3c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left\\lvert (-2) \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot (-2) + 1 \\cdot 0 - 0 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot 1 - (-2) \\cdot (-2)\\right\\rvert = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left\\lvert -12\\right\\rvert = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~6}.\\]\" width=\"606\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_1.2_.28alternative_using_triangle_in_-plane_for_last_two_steps.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.2_(alternative_using_triangle_in_-plane_for_last_two_steps)\">Solution 1.2 (alternative using triangle in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-plane for last two steps)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.2 (alternative using triangle in -plane for last two steps)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>As an alternative to arguing that the triangle formed by the three roots for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> has the same area as the one originally formed by the roots for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, we can simply convert back to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-plane using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/8/a886af86c209586c9941d381b2323bcfb5b92a4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z = w-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" />. Therefore, as the roots for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0b8009df368d0bdeb0e40a697c49babde402094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf1294c23fe3c251dfda2bdcf0240c9822fac7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, those for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/0/c008e2d9c20a61957df4cea994f5e8d4a5b99df4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6c9270fea5f65a61b9acfb99b3b0107a8e141f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1+2i)-2i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/656bc5cb85f304f802e5fb9975e403a8cb3349d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-2i)-2i = 1-4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"171\" height=\"20\" />, with the vertical side of the triangle thus being that between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb5c937a228f739dbd0b504a804b81b49e07b49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>We can now use either of the two approaches above: for the former, the vertical side again has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/0/f706c28c0616b628c6f96737862da84ae8b239e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0-(-4) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" /> and the corresponding altitude has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/b/77b2468395eef65bdd7ab1f4624bb21423dad508.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(-2) = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />, once again giving the area as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfd7edb7a737843d03df1c08a28866c1a1d1aad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"139\" height=\"38\" />. For the latter, the shoelace formula also gives the area as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/a/52afe41110436e5f21cb7e4f5641f1dc9509690e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left\\lvert (-2) \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot (-2) - (-2) \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot 1 - (-4) \\cdot (-2)\\right\\rvert = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left\\lvert -12\\right\\rvert = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~6}.\\]\" width=\"662\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>~backtosq-1\n~minor edit by 526\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28directly_finding_the_roots.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(directly_finding_the_roots)\">Solution 2 (directly finding the roots)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (directly finding the roots)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Expanding the given polynomial yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/d/19d67c881233d2a0f273e19f363e28a41d282c7d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}(z+i)(z+2i)(z+3i)+10 &amp;= \\left(z^2 + 2iz + iz + 2 \\cdot (-1)\\right)\\left(z+3i\\right)+10 \\\\ &amp;= \\left(z^2+3iz-2\\right)\\left(z+3i\\right)+10 \\\\ &amp;= z^3 + 3iz^2 + 3iz^2 + 9 \\cdot (-1)z - 2z - 6i + 10 \\\\ &amp;= \\left(z^3-11z+10\\right)+\\left(6z^2-6\\right)i,\\end{align*}\" width=\"571\" height=\"104\" /> and so any complex number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> will be a root if and only if it is a root of both the real part, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/6/0b6344b314ebcd94e540d8a21806980c49dee437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(z^3-11z+10\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"125\" height=\"23\" />, and the imaginary part, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/c/eec20c8818e61022a940bd5c6b90a0c625566185.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(6z^2-6\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"75\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>We immediately observe that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed a root of both of them, so by factoring out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c63470ad34c18869d3dc0a0bbd4c53100fd6ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/f/41f1103f58b05add062780b236ef04dd1987e81e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+i)(z+2i)(z+3i)+10 = \\left(z-1\\right)\\left(z^2+(1+6i)z+(-10+6i)\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"530\" height=\"23\" />, and now using the quadratic formula, we deduce that the other two roots are given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c137db51c0098f8a7c8ac453317e12c798aa1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}z &amp;= \\frac{-(1+6i) \\pm \\sqrt{(1+6i)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-10+6i)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{-1-6i \\pm \\sqrt{1+12i-36+40-24i}}{2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{-1-6i \\pm \\sqrt{5-12i}}{2}.\\end{align*}\" width=\"369\" height=\"135\" />\n</p><p>Noting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/a/1aaa180eaaa078f20c52aaa59125aa2b2efb950d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5-12i = (3-2i)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"150\" height=\"21\" /> (or else by writing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/4/b34c6987e373665de920be6f47deed8fa6ffdc28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5-12i = (a+bi)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"21\" /> for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/f/53fb763d3b5e70439c3c2f095e756e90ea5d6a5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />, and then solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> by comparing real and imaginary parts of both sides), the above expression reduces to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/b/edbfa22a6d5e03096d60254531eea0fe58a9cd03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z = \\frac{-1-6i+3-2i}{2} = \\frac{2-8i}{2} = 1-4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"315\" height=\"39\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bddf085092ab33bfcecf688989e8f26b6060ac78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z = \\frac{-1-6i-3+2i}{2} = \\frac{-4-4i}{2} = -2-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"343\" height=\"39\" />. In other words, the three roots are precisely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb5c937a228f739dbd0b504a804b81b49e07b49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/0/c008e2d9c20a61957df4cea994f5e8d4a5b99df4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2-2i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" />, and thus using either of the approaches in Solution 1.2 above, the area of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/a/6daa08263129471ce197acf9a06ce4faae9b600d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"57\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ScoutViolet and ~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WIkOFDf3Fr4\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WIkOFDf3Fr4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XbM3xlwy4S8\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XbM3xlwy4S8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_Continuum_Math\">Video Solution 3 by Continuum Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by Continuum Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=esnkV01caOw\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=esnkV01caOw</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_4_by_MadMath\">Video Solution 4 by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 4 by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/PerM9J-xfGA\">https://youtu.be/PerM9J-xfGA</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fast_Video_Solution_by_Veer_Mahajan\">Fast Video Solution by Veer Mahajan</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Fast Video Solution by Veer Mahajan\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/4Gn0brFsnco\">https://youtu.be/4Gn0brFsnco</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A\">2025 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nof